WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.897
Project A uses subqueries to build a patient-level feature table.

00:00:03.897 --> 00:00:09.777
Each patient receives an admission count, abnormal-lab count, and distinct-medication count.

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:13.802
The query then combines those features into a simple teaching score.

00:00:13.802 --> 00:00:16.147
The score is not a clinical model.

00:00:16.147 --> 00:00:22.237
The SQL lesson is to create features at one clear grain and then calculate a derived value from them.

00:00:22.237 --> 00:00:27.452
Connect this idea to an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:32.442
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:39.222
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.222 --> 00:00:44.557
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.557 --> 00:00:49.840
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
